fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1047", description: some(value: "`|` (DISJUNCTION) ANSWERS NOTHING THROUGH THE `anthill query` CLI WHILE WORKING IN-PROCESS — the same rule, the same file, two different answers depending on which entry point loads it.\n\nMEASURED, one file, no imports (`fact l(1)`, `fact r(2)`, `rule either(?x) :- l(?x) | r(?x)`):\n  in-process (`common::load_kb_with` + `query_unary`)         -> 2 solutions   (CORRECT)\n  `anthill query --path <file> \"demo.q1.either(?x)\"`          -> \"no solutions\"\nCONTROLS, same file, same CLI invocation:\n  `rule both(?x) :- l(?x), r(?y)`                             -> 1 solution    (the comma form is fine)\n  `rule viaPush(?x) :- push_choice(l(?x), r(?x))`             -> 2 solutions   (the PRIMITIVE is fine)\n  `rule viaKernelOr(?x) :- anthill.kernel.or(l(?x), r(?x))`   -> \"no solutions\"\nSo it is not the file, not the query pattern, and not `push_choice`: it is the kernel `or` RULE (`stdlib/anthill/kernel/kernel.anthill:48`, `rule or(?a, ?b) :- push_choice(?a, ?b)`) failing to fire under the CLI. Naming it fully qualified does not help, and the name RESOLVES — an unresolvable functor gets the loud WI-754 report instead (\"does not resolve to a known functor\"), which this does not.\n\nNOT CAUSED BY WI-1046, verified by `git stash`: the same \"no solutions\" is produced by the commit before it. WI-1046's routing degrades safely here — it maps to `anthill.kernel.or` only when that qualified name exists.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY RULED OUT: `kernel/kernel.anthill` IS in the CLI's embedded set (`anthill-stl/src/stdlib.rs:109`), so a missing FILE is not the explanation. The remaining candidates are that its clause is not asserted under the CLI's load path, or that the CLI's query/resolve configuration does not reach a clause whose body binds goal-valued variables.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: this is the silent-wrong-answer class, at the entry point users actually type. A disjunctive rule loads clean, is reported by `anthill check`, and answers nothing — with no diagnostic. The in-process suite cannot see it, which is why it survived: every disjunction test in the tree drives the KB directly.\n\nWORK: find which of the two candidates it is (compare the CLI's assembled KB against the in-process one for the `or` clause — `rules_by_functor(anthill.kernel.or)` on both), then fix. ACCEPTANCE: the table above re-measured with the CLI row answering 2; the three CONTROL rows unchanged; a CLI-level test (`anthill-cli/tests/`) driving a disjunctive rule end-to-end, since an in-process test provably cannot catch this; each test says which case fails when the change is backed out. `cargo-test` green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-1046 (which found it); WI-075 / proposal 033 (`push_choice`); `stdlib/anthill/kernel/kernel.anthill:48`."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-07T23:02:30Z"))
